+    /* MUST_WAIT: do not leave the scan head travelling when the scan ends.
+
+       Without it genesys issues the park command and returns immediately
+       (`move_back_home(dev, false)`), so `scanimage` exits while the head is
+       still moving.  A moving OpticFilm does not answer control transfers.
+       On Linux and macOS that costs a retry; on Windows the still-image class
+       driver waits its own two minutes and then fails the transfer with
+       ERROR_SEM_TIMEOUT (121), which surfaces as
+
+           open of device genesys:usbscan:000 failed: Error during device I/O
+
+       in the *next* scan, or as `sane_read: Error during device I/O` in the
+       current one -- `sane_read` parks at EOF, so a parking failure throws
+       away a scan whose data was already complete.
+
+       Measured on an OpticFilm 8100, scanning 600/1200/2400/3600/7200 dpi in
+       sequence: back to back, 2 of 3 runs failed this way; with an 8 s idle
+       gap between scans, 15 of 15 succeeded.  The gap is what the head needs
+       to reach home.
+
+       No model in 1.4.0 sets this flag, so upstream never exercises the path.
+       It is small and bounded: `sane_read` stops parking at EOF, and
+       `sane_cancel` parks with `wait_until_home`, which polls the home sensor
+       300 times at 100 ms and gives up after 30 s.  */
     model.flags = ModelFlag::WARMUP |
                   ModelFlag::CUSTOM_GAMMA |
                   ModelFlag::DARK_CALIBRATION |
-                  ModelFlag::SHADING_REPARK;
+                  ModelFlag::SHADING_REPARK |
+                  ModelFlag::MUST_WAIT;
 
     model.search_lines = 200;
     s_usb_devices->emplace_back(0x07b3, 0x0807, model);
